1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,200
Producing enough energy to meet our needs has become a serious problem.

2
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:15,190
Demand is rising rapidly, because of the world's increasing population and expanding industry.

3
00:00:15,190 --> 00:00:18,140
Burning fossil fuels, like gas

4
00:00:18,360 --> 00:00:24,850
coal and oil, seriously damages the environment and they'll eventually run out.

5
00:00:24,850 --> 00:00:27,120
For a number of years now

6
00:00:27,300 --> 00:00:33,251
scientists have been working out how we can derive energy from renewable sources,

7
00:00:33,251 --> 00:00:37,230
such as the sun and wind, without causing pollution.

8
00:00:37,230 --> 00:00:40,946
Today I'll outline marine renewable energy -

9
00:00:40,946 --> 00:00:47,760
also called ocean energy - which harnesses the movement of the oceans.

10
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:53,724
Marine renewable energy can be divided into three main categories:

11
00:00:53,724 --> 00:01:00,380
wave energy, tidal energy and ocean thermal energy conversion

12
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:04,460
and I'll say a few words about each one.

13
00:01:04,460 --> 00:01:12,080
First, wave energy. Numerous devices have been invented to harvest wave energy

14
00:01:12,080 --> 00:01:13,649
with names such as Wave Dragon,

15
00:01:13,649 --> 00:01:18,260
the Penguin and Mighty Whale

16
00:01:18,560 --> 00:01:23,860
and research is going on to try and come up with a really efficient method.

17
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:27,310
This form of energy has plenty of potential,

18
00:01:27,310 --> 00:01:33,780
as the source is constant, and there's no danger of waves coming to a standstill.

19
00:01:33,780 --> 00:01:39,060
Electricity can be generated using onshore systems

20
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:43,470
using a reservoir, or offshore systems.

21
00:01:43,470 --> 00:01:47,920
But the problem with ocean waves is that they're erratic

22
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:52,570
with the wind making them travel in every direction.

23
00:01:52,570 --> 00:01:57,622
This adds to the difficulty of creating efficient technology:

24
00:01:57,622 --> 00:02:01,720
ideally all the waves would travel smoothly

25
00:02:01,720 --> 00:02:05,480
and regularly along the same straight line.

26
00:02:05,480 --> 00:02:08,300
Another drawback is that sand

27
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:14,592
and other sediment on the ocean floor might be stopped from flowing normally,

28
00:02:14,592 --> 00:02:17,630
which can lead to environmental problems.

29
00:02:17,630 --> 00:02:24,000
The second category of marine energy that I'll mention is tidal energy.

30
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:29,280
One major advantage of using the tide, rather than waves

31
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:32,901
as a source of energy is that it's predictable:

32
00:02:32,901 --> 00:02:39,460
we know the exact times of high and low tides for years to come.

33
00:02:39,460 --> 00:02:42,420
For tidal energy to be effective

34
00:02:43,080 --> 00:02:49,815
the difference between high and low tides needs to be at least five metres,

35
00:02:49,815 --> 00:02:55,510
and this occurs naturally in only about forty places on Earth.

36
00:02:55,510 --> 00:03:01,744
But the right conditions can be created by constructing a tidal lagoon,

37
00:03:01,744 --> 00:03:06,420
an area of sea water separated from the sea.

38
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:12,870
One current plan is to create a tidal lagoon on the coast of Wales.

39
00:03:12,870 --> 00:03:18,710
This will be an area of water within a bay at Swansea,

40
00:03:18,710 --> 00:03:24,550
sheltered by a U-shaped breakwater, or dam, built out from the coast.

41
00:03:24,550 --> 00:03:31,861
The breakwater will contain sixteen hydro turbines, and as the tide rises,

42
00:03:31,861 --> 00:03:36,860
water rushes through the breakwater, activating the turbines

43
00:03:37,260 --> 00:03:41,000
which turn a generator to produce electricity.

44
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:44,787
Then, for three hours as the tide goes out,

45
00:03:44,787 --> 00:03:48,160
the water is held back within the breakwater

46
00:03:48,660 --> 00:03:51,099
increasing the difference in water level,

47
00:03:51,099 --> 00:03:56,570
until it's several metres higher within the lagoon than in the open sea.

48
00:03:56,570 --> 00:04:04,600
Then, in order to release the stored water, gates in the breakwater are opened.

49
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:08,020
It pours powerfully out of the lagoon

50
00:04:08,380 --> 00:04:13,508
driving the turbines in the breakwater in the opposite direction

51
00:04:13,508 --> 00:04:17,700
and again generating thousands of megawatts of electricity.

52
00:04:17,700 --> 00:04:21,620
As there are two high tides a day

53
00:04:22,020 --> 00:04:27,420
this lagoon scheme would generate electricity four times a day,

54
00:04:27,420 --> 00:04:31,800
every day, for a total of around 14 hours in every 24

55
00:04:32,540 --> 00:04:38,220
and enough electricity for over 150,000 homes.

56
00:04:38,220 --> 00:04:41,520
This system has quite a lot in its favour

57
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:46,573
unlike solar and wind energy it doesn't depend on the weather;

58
00:04:46,573 --> 00:04:49,820
the turbines are operated without the need for fuel

59
00:04:50,180 --> 00:04:53,674
so it doesn't create any greenhouse gas emissions;

60
00:04:53,674 --> 00:04:56,180
and very little maintenance is needed.

61
00:04:56,180 --> 00:05:01,859
It's estimated that electricity generated in this way will be relatively cheap,

62
00:05:01,859 --> 00:05:07,800
and that manufacturing the components would create more than 2,000 jobs

63
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:10,850
a big boost to the local economy.

64
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:18,865
On the other hand, there are fears that lagoons might harm both fish and birds,

65
00:05:18,865 --> 00:05:21,280
for example by disturbing migration patterns

66
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:26,880
and causing a build-up of silt, affecting local ecosystems.

67
00:05:26,880 --> 00:05:30,364
There are other forms of tidal energy,

68
00:05:30,364 --> 00:05:37,830
but I'll go on to the third category of marine energy: ocean thermal energy conversion.

69
00:05:37,830 --> 00:05:44,339
This depends on there being a big difference in temperature between surface water

70
00:05:44,339 --> 00:05:48,040
and the water a couple of kilometres below the surface

71
00:05:48,680 --> 00:05:52,850
and this occurs in tropical coastal areas.

72
00:05:52,850 --> 00:06:00,000
The idea is to bring cold water up to the surface using a submerged pipe.

73
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:05,690
